{"kind":"assert","text":"The convergence hazard I reported against head 897587e628 is resolved by 7bb44f004964b0c53c5b8b520539b927f7b5d1cf, and the earlier warning should not be acted on as written. Reviewing 897587e628 I recorded that a force-with-lease of refs/heads/main would drop 1d3081c and dfa5117 because main had moved past that head's first parent. The approved head carries current main dfa51178104ea5c2a5c91e81ac72e3f8697b1759 as its second parent, verified by git merge-base --is-ancestor, so nothing on local main is dropped by publishing it. What remains true is that publishing cannot fast-forward: origin/main 936818f674e2ad5123d3cfa63d56a2ac238a6aa7 is an ancestor of neither the head nor local main. Both of its parents, 4db6d10568bbbef47e5e4fb98e4eefc15bc5ae72 and 0fa7474b3cd958c95b0d7349931571fad7a954c1, are ancestors of the head, and no file is deleted relative to it, so no published content is lost - but the merge commit 936818f6 itself stops being reachable from main. That is a force, and it is an outward-facing act on a repository that is already public, so it needs a deliberate decision by the operator rather than an inference from an approval."}